1091
General Jurisdiction
We now turn our attention from one mode of personal jurisdiction, known as specific jurisdiction, to the other, known as general jurisdiction. To recap in the words of International Shoe, under the “minimum contacts” framework, defendants who are “not present within the territory of the forum” may be subject to suit in the forum state if either the claims against the defendant “arise out of or are connected with the [defendant’s] activities within the state” (specific jurisdiction), or the defendant’s activities “within a state [are] so substantial … as to justify suit against it on causes of action arising from dealings entirely distinct from those activities” (general jurisdiction).

So far, we have focused on specific jurisdiction, which may allow a court to exercise personal jurisdiction over the defendant when the plaintiff’s claim arises out of the defendant’s contacts with the forum state. Specific jurisdiction is not a basis for personal jurisdiction when the plaintiff’s claims do not arise out of the defendant’s contacts with the forum state, even if those contacts are extensive.

Extensive contacts with the forum state can, however, form the basis for general jurisdiction. The connection between the defendant and the forum state may be so strong that the defendant is subject to general jurisdiction, which means that the courts of the forum state have personal jurisdiction over the defendant for any and all claims that a plaintiff may bring—even claims totally unrelated to the defendant’s activities in the forum state. The key question for general jurisdiction is this: How substantial must the defendant’s connection to the forum state be for the defendant to be subject to general jurisdiction there? After remaining essentially silent on this question for decades, the Supreme Court began to mark the limits of general jurisdiction in Goodyear Dunlop Tires v. Brown and Daimler v. Bauman.

But before we turn our attention to Goodyear and Daimler, we come full circle to Pennoyer. The “minimum contacts” framework of International Shoe applies only to defendants “not present within the territory of the forum.” For defendants who are present within the forum when served with process, American courts have long cited Pennoyer for the rule that a defendant’s physical presence within the territory of the forum state at the time of service is sufficient for the courts of that state to exercise personal jurisdiction over the defendant. This is
1092
known colloquially as “tag jurisdiction” and allows the forum court to hear any claims against the defendant, even claims with no connection to the forum state.

Although International Shoe did not purport to say anything about constitutional limits on the exercise of jurisdiction over defendants served within the forum state, its emphasis on fairness as a central principle for personal jurisdiction raises the question whether tag jurisdiction is “fair”—at least in the sense required by due process. Our next case, Burnham v. Superior Court, considers this question.

Burnham v. Superior Court of California
_________________________

495 U.S. 604 (1990)

JUSTICE SCALIA announced the judgment of the Court and delivered an opinion in which THE CHIEF JUSTICE and JUSTICE KENNEDY join, and in which JUSTICE WHITE joins with respect to Parts I, II-A, II-B, and II-C.

The question presented is whether the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment denies California courts jurisdiction over a nonresident, who was personally served with process while temporarily in that State, in a suit unrelated to his activities in the State.

I

Petitioner Dennis Burnham married Francie Burnham in 1976 in West Virginia. In 1977 the couple moved to New Jersey, where their two children were born. In July 1987 the Burnhams decided to separate. They agreed that Mrs. Burnham, who intended to move to California, would take custody of the children. Shortly before Mrs. Burnham departed for California that same month, she and petitioner agreed that she would file for divorce on grounds of “irreconcilable differences.”

In October 1987, petitioner filed for divorce in New Jersey state court on grounds of “desertion.” Petitioner did not, however, obtain an issuance of summons against his wife and did not attempt to serve her with process. Mrs. Burnham, after unsuccessfully demanding that petitioner adhere to their prior agreement to submit to an “irreconcilable differences” divorce, brought suit for divorce in California state court in early January 1988.
